<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <!-- net8.0: [DynamicallyAccessedMembers], JsonSerializerContext, IsTrimmable APIs.
             netstandard2.1: Unity IL2CPP / older .NET consumers. STJ is a PackageReference there. -->
        <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;netstandard2.1</TargetFrameworks>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <LangVersion>9.0</LangVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <PropertyGroup>
        <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
        <PackageId>Datra</PackageId>
        <Description>Datra is a game data management system that uses C# Source Generators to automatically generate serialization code for CSV, JSON, and YAML data. Works seamlessly in both Unity and .NET environments.</Description>
        <PackageTags>game;data;serialization;csv;json;yaml;source-generator;unity</PackageTags>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        Let the trimmer trim this.

        ILLink only trims assemblies that opt in here; everything else gets the
        `copy` action, and a copied assembly stays in the publish output even
        when nothing references it any more. Without this, a Blazor WebAssembly
        consumer that never calls Datra still shipped Datra.dll (347 KB) and,
        through it, YamlDotNet (293 KB — measured at 293,141 bytes on the wire
        against 293,376 in the package: a 235-byte difference, so it had never
        been trimmed at all).

        That is also why the two previous attempts at this did nothing.
        DatraEmitYamlSerializers and the lazily-created YAML serializer both
        reduce REFERENCES, and references were never what kept these assemblies
        in the output.

        net8.0 only: netstandard2.1 exists for Unity/IL2CPP, which has its own
        stripping story and does not read this.

        The metadata and the analyzer are separate switches, and only the first
        is being turned on here. IsTrimmable is a statement to the CONSUMER's
        ILLink: you may trim me. EnableTrimAnalyzer would be a statement to this
        build, and it currently produces 114 warnings (58 IL2026, 30 IL2067,
        20 IL2070, 6 IL2046) across the reflection-based YAML, the reflection
        JSON path and the provider interfaces. Every one of them is true, and
        annotating them is real work that is not this change; 114 warnings on
        every build would be read by nobody. The paths a consumer can actually
        reach are already declared [RequiresUnreferencedCode], which is the
        contract that matters to them, and their publish still collapses ours
        into the usual one-warning-per-assembly notice.

        The 6 IL2046 are worth a separate look when someone does that work:
        IDataProvider's Load/SaveAsync are unannotated while the implementations
        are annotated, so a caller holding the interface is told nothing.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'">
        <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
        <EnableTrimAnalyzer>false</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
      <PackageReference Include="YamlDotNet" Version="16.3.0" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        YamlDotNet does not set IsTrimmable either, so it survives on its own
        once Datra is gone. A consumer can say so, but only if it knows to —
        this package knows, so it offers the switch.

        Off by default and it has to be: trimming YamlDotNet is safe exactly
        when nothing parses YAML, because its deserializer is reflection-driven
        and a trimmed build that does read YAML fails rather than running slow.
        A JSON/CSV-only consumer sets <DatraTrimYaml>true</DatraTrimYaml> and
        gets both assemblies back off the wire.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
      <!-- buildTransitive as well as build: NuGet flows `build/` assets only to
           the project that names the PackageReference, and Datra is usually a
           transitive dependency — a game references the framework, the framework
           references Datra, and the switch has to reach the application, which
           is the project that publishes and therefore the only one that trims.
           Shipping only `build/` is why this silently did nothing on the first
           attempt. -->
      <None Include="build/Datra.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="build/" />
      <None Include="build/Datra.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="buildTransitive/" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!-- netstandard2.1: STJ is a separate package. net8 has it in BCL. -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.1'">
      <PackageReference Include="System.Text.Json" Version="8.0.5" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
